# OKF security pipeline — whitelist repo.
# /opt/ares is the deployed runtime: nested project clones, runtime state,
# evidence dirs and key files all live here and all stay OUT of git.
# Everything is ignored except the pipeline modules and their documentation.
/*

# pipeline modules (this repo's entire scope)
!/.gitignore
!/okf_agent_stamp.py
!/okf_anchor.py
!/okf_api.py
!/okf_btc_chain.py
!/okf_clock_mcp.py
!/okf_evasion.py
!/okf_memory.py
!/okf_now.py
!/okf_ots_upgrade.sh
!/okf_ots_verify.py
!/okf_outbox.py
!/okf_pipeline.py
!/okf_pin.py
!/okf_receipt.py
!/okf_time_attest.py
!/okf_tool_manifests.py
!/tool_manifests.json
!/koodu_clock.py
!/koodu_time.py
!/strix_pipeline_v4.py
!/stix_webhook.py
!/stix_ingester.py
!/stix_scanner.py
!/strix_scan.sh
!/betterleaks_scan.sh

# documentation
!/docs

# pipeline history + profile (same repo's scope, was untracked)
!/okf_profile.py
!/okf_sid_anchor.py
!/strix_pipeline.py
!/strix_pipeline_v2.py
!/strix_pipeline_v3.py
!/strix_runner.py

# the pipeline's own state and evidence — "ALL of it" (2026-11-10)
# receipts + sidecars, sealed evidence reports, and the verified Bitcoin
# ledger snapshot. Keys stay excluded: the whitelist model means a dotfile
# like .okf_anchor_key is ignored unless named here, and it is not named.
!/security_receipts
!/security_reports
!/.btc_anchor.json
!/.btc_headers.json
!/.btc_pins.json
!/deploy

# planted canary credentials (a fake private-key block, among others) prove
# the scanner works; they must keep looking like secrets to every future
# audit, so the raw logs stay on disk but never enter git. The receipts record that the test passed.
security_reports/scan-canary_*/raw/betterleaks.log
